+## Authorized HTTP workflows, including device pairing
+
+A read-only capability's method restriction is not a ban on the operator's authorized task.
+When the objective includes pairing or another HTTP write, request `allow_state_changing_http`
+at Hunt start, reuse standing target authorization, and leave `capabilities` empty unless the
+operator asked to narrow it. Do not copy the read-only example's false write flag into that task.
+
+Use `http.request_active` for POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE with exactly one `json_body`, `form_body`, or
+`raw_body` when needed. It uses the same pinned worker, selected principals, alternate-service
+origins, HTTP/self-signed HTTPS support, receipts and existing budgets. It returns redirects
+without repeating a write. Do not use a separate shell command or disguise a write as GET.
+Secrets belong in managed credential profiles or saved request collections, not body inputs;
+a selected profile/session must already permit the capability being used.
+
+Reuse the same idempotency key only for the identical action after a lost response. A new key
+means a new action and may repeat the device's side effect. Check the retained receipt and actual
+device state before retrying an uncertain result. HTTP success alone does not prove pairing.
+If an existing run lacks write authority, start a correctly configured Hunt using the same
+standing authorization; do not repeatedly ask for the same consent or invent permission. If the
+live server predates these capabilities, name the implementation/version gap rather than claiming
+the operator is unauthorized. WebSocket pairing still needs a compatible protocol executor.
 
